{"name":"Milk Brownie Dough","hex":"#8f7265","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"milk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A white liquid produced by the mammary glands of female mammals to nourish their young. From certain animals, especially cows, it is also called dairy milk and is a common food for humans as a beverage or used to produce various dairy products such as butter, cheese, and yogurt.","A white (or whitish) liquid obtained from a vegetable source such as almonds, coconuts, oats, rice, or soy beans."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To express milk from (a mammal, especially a cow).","To draw (milk) from the breasts or udder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/milk"},{"term":"brownie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small square piece of rich cake, usually made with chocolate. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A sweet bread with brown sugar and currants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brownie"},{"term":"dough","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick, malleable substance made by mixing flour with other ingredients such as water, eggs, or butter, that is made into a particular form and then baked.","Money."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make into dough."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dough"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brownie (folklore)","blocks":[{"text":"A brownie or broonie (Scots), also known as a brùnaidh or gruagach, is a household spirit or hobgoblin from Scottish folklore that is said to come out at night while the owners of the house are asleep and perform various chores and farming tasks. The human owners of the house must leave a bowl of milk or cream or some other offering for the brownie, usually by the hearth. Brownies are described as easily offended and will leave their homes forever if they feel they have been insulted or in any way taken advantage of. Brownies are characteristically mischievous and are often said to punish or pull pranks on lazy servants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Illustration_of_a_brownie_by_Alice_B_Woodward.jpg/330px-Illustration_of_a_brownie_by_Alice_B_Woodward.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownie_(folklore)","wikidata":"Q1783949"},{"title":"Milk","blocks":[{"text":"Milk is usually a white liquid food produced by the mammary glands of lactating mammals. It is the primary source of nutrition for young mammals before they are able to digest solid food. Milk contains many nutrients, including calcium and protein, as well as lactose and saturated fat; the enzyme lactase is needed to break down lactose. Immune factors and immune-modulating components in milk contribute to milk immunity. The first milk, which is called colostrum, contains antibodies and immune-modulating components that strengthen the immune system against many diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk","wikidata":"Q8495"},{"title":"Dough","blocks":[{"text":"Dough is a malleable, sometimes elastic paste most commonly made from flour or meal. Dough is typically made by mixing flour with a small amount of water or other liquid, and sometimes includes yeast, other leavening agents, or ingredients such as fats or flavourings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Dough.jpg/330px-Dough.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dough","wikidata":"Q178024"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Vettii.jpg/500px-Vettii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Vettii.jpg/1280px-Vettii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Vettii.jpg/500px-Vettii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman Lararium, or household shrine to the Lares, from the House of the Vettii in Pompeii. Brownies bear many similarities to the Roman Lares.[2][3][4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVettii.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/GrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg/500px-GrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/GrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg/1280px-GrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/GrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg/500px-GrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A recurring folkloric motif holds that, if presented with clothing, a brownie will leave his family forever and never work for them again,[14][31][32] similar to the Wichtelmänner in the German story of \"The Elves and the Shoemaker\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrimmsGoblins-090-TheElvesAndTheShoemaker.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Illustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg/500px-Illustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Illustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg/1280px-Illustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Illustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg/500px-Illustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"O Waken, Waken, Burd Isbel, illustration by Arthur Rackham to Young Bekie, showing Billy Blind waking Burd Isobel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_to_the_ballad_Young_Beckie_from_%22Some_British_Ballads%22.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Covenanters_in_a_Glen.jpg/500px-Covenanters_in_a_Glen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Covenanters_in_a_Glen.jpg/500px-Covenanters_in_a_Glen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In James Hogg's 1818 novel The Brownie of Bodsbeck, the eponymous \"brownie\" turns out to be John Brown, the leader of the Covenanters, a persecuted Scottish Presbyterian movement.[69] An illegal meeting of Covenanters is shown in this painting, Covenanters in a Glen by Alexander Carse.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACovenanters_in_a_Glen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Palmer_Cox-Brownie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Palmer_Cox-Brownie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"An illustration of a brownie by Palmer Cox from his Brownies Around the World (1894).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalmer_Cox-Brownie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com